### Rapid Inductive Switching & Clamping Test Sketch

Let's test rapid inductive load switching. When running this sketch, the flyback diode (such as a 1N4007 or 1N4148) across the motor terminals guarantees that fast digital transitions on Pin 9 do not feed destructive back-EMF spikes into your board.

```cpp
// Inductive Load Switching Test Sketch
// Verifies safe switching when protected by a flyback clamp diode
const int motorPin = 9;

void setup() {
  pinMode(motorPin, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(motorPin, LOW); // Start with de-energized coil

  Serial.begin(9600);
  Serial.println(F("--- Flyback Protection Verification Test ---"));
  Serial.println(F("Flyback diode connected antiparallel across motor terminals"));
}

void loop() {
  // Energize coil: current ramps up, establishing magnetic field
  Serial.println(F("COIL CHARGING: Transistor ON -> Storing magnetic energy (1/2 L I^2)"));
  digitalWrite(motorPin, HIGH);
  delay(1000);

  // Abrupt switch off: di/dt causes instant back-EMF kickback
  Serial.println(F("COIL DISCHARGING: Transistor OFF -> Diode safely recirculates spike!"));
  digitalWrite(motorPin, LOW);
  delay(2000); // Allow complete inductive energy decay
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
- `digitalWrite(motorPin, HIGH)`: Closes the driver switch. Current flows through the motor winding, building a magnetic field in the coil.
- `digitalWrite(motorPin, LOW)`: Opens the driver switch in under 1 microsecond. Without the flyback diode, a 70V spike would arc across the transistor. With the diode installed, the spike is clamped to ~5.7V and current decays harmlessly in the diode loop.
- `delay(2000)`: Ensures that the collapsing magnetic field completely dissipates before the next cycle begins.
